# Token Tach — Cursor Rules
# AI credit/cost tachometer: Tauri v2 + Rust + Svelte 5 + TypeScript

# ============================================================================
# PROJECT IDENTITY
# ============================================================================
# Token Tach is a macOS menubar/dock utility that monitors AI coding tool spend
# in real time. Three-layer UI: tray numbers, hover breakdown, click for full
# translucent HUD. Built with Tauri v2 (Rust backend) + Svelte 5 (frontend).
#
# Supported providers: Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf, Copilot, Codex, Kilo Code
# Stack: Tauri v2, Rust, Svelte 5, TypeScript, SQLite, OS Keychain

# ============================================================================
# TDD — TEST-DRIVEN DEVELOPMENT (NON-NEGOTIABLE)
# ============================================================================
# Every feature follows Red-Green-Refactor. No exceptions.
#
# 1. RED:    Write a failing test FIRST. Do not write implementation code
#            without a corresponding test already in place.
# 2. GREEN:  Write the MINIMUM code to make the test pass. No gold-plating.
# 3. REFACTOR: Clean up while all tests stay green. No behavior changes.
#
# Test file naming:
#   Rust:       src/<module>/tests.rs or tests/<module>_test.rs
#   Svelte/TS:  src/lib/<module>/<module>.test.ts
#   E2E:        tests/e2e/<feature>.test.ts
#
# Test requirements:
# - Every public function MUST have at least one test
# - Every provider adapter MUST have integration tests with mock responses
# - Every Svelte component MUST have a component test
# - Every Tauri command MUST have a unit test on the Rust side
# - Edge cases: null/empty inputs, error states, boundary values
# - Tests MUST be independent — no shared mutable state between tests
# - Tests MUST be deterministic — no flaky tests, no sleep-based waits
# - Name tests descriptively: test_<unit>_<scenario>_<expected_outcome>
#
# When asked to implement a feature:
#   1. Ask: "What should the test look like?"
#   2. Write the test file first
#   3. Run the test — confirm it fails
#   4. Implement until green
#   5. Refactor
#   6. Never skip step 1
#
# Coverage target: 80% minimum. 90%+ for provider adapters and core logic.

# ============================================================================
# SOLID PRINCIPLES + INTERFACE SEGREGATION (ISPF)
# ============================================================================
# S — Single Responsibility
#   - One struct/module = one reason to change
#   - Provider adapter: ONLY fetches usage data. Does NOT format, store, or display.
#   - UI component: ONLY renders. Does NOT fetch data or manage state.
#   - Storage layer: ONLY persists. Does NOT validate or transform.
#
# O — Open/Closed
#   - New providers are added by implementing the ProviderAdapter trait.
#     NEVER modify existing adapter code to add a new provider.
#   - UI themes/views extend via composition, not modification.
#
# L — Liskov Substitution
#   - Any ProviderAdapter implementation MUST be swappable without breaking
#     the polling engine or UI. If it implements the trait, it works.
#   - Mock adapters in tests MUST behave identically to real ones
#     (same error types, same data shapes).
#
# I — Interface Segregation (STRICT ENFORCEMENT)
#   - Traits MUST be small and focused. No god-traits.
#   - Split capabilities:
#       trait UsageFetcher    { fn fetch_usage(&self) -> Result<Usage>; }
#       trait BalanceChecker  { fn check_balance(&self) -> Result<Balance>; }
#       trait BurnRateCalc    { fn burn_rate(&self) -> Result<Rate>; }
#   - A provider that doesn't support balance checking does NOT implement
#     BalanceChecker. It is NOT forced to stub it out.
#   - Frontend: component props MUST be minimal. Pass only what the component
#     needs. Never pass an entire provider object when only name + rate needed.
#   - Tauri commands: one command per action. No multi-purpose "do_thing"
#     commands with mode flags.
#
# D — Dependency Inversion
#   - Core logic depends on traits, NEVER on concrete provider implementations.
#   - The polling engine accepts Vec<Box<dyn UsageFetcher>>, not
#     Vec<AnthropicAdapter>.
#   - Frontend fetches data via a typed API layer, not raw Tauri invoke calls.
#   - Storage is accessed through a Repository trait, not direct SQLite calls.

# ============================================================================
# RUST RULES (src-tauri/)
# ============================================================================
# - Edition 2021, stable toolchain
# - Use thiserror for error types, anyhow in application code only (not libs)
# - Every public type and function has a doc comment
# - No unwrap() in production code. Use expect() only with descriptive messages
#   for truly impossible states. Prefer ? operator everywhere.
# - No unsafe unless absolutely required and documented with a SAFETY comment
# - Use #[cfg(test)] mod tests in the same file for unit tests
# - Async: use tokio with Tauri's async runtime. No blocking calls on main thread.
# - Secrets: NEVER log, print, serialize, or store API keys in plaintext.
#   Access exclusively through the keyring crate.
# - Provider adapters:
#   - Each provider in its own module: src/providers/<name>/mod.rs
#   - Implement the trait(s) it supports
#   - Include a mock.rs for testing with realistic fixture data
#   - Parse API responses into internal types — no leaking external API shapes
# - Error handling: provider errors MUST be typed (RateLimit, AuthFailed,
#   NetworkError, etc.) — not string messages
# - All HTTP requests MUST have timeouts (30s default)
# - Polling engine MUST handle individual provider failures gracefully —
#   one provider down does NOT stop others

# ============================================================================
# SVELTE 5 + TYPESCRIPT RULES (src/)
# ============================================================================
# - Svelte 5 runes syntax ($state, $derived, $effect) — no legacy stores
# - TypeScript strict mode, no `any` types
# - Components:
#   - One component per file
#   - Props via $props() rune with TypeScript interface
#   - Keep components under 150 lines — extract sub-components
#   - Presentation components: no side effects, no data fetching
#   - Container components: manage data flow, invoke Tauri commands
# - Styling:
#   - Scoped <style> blocks in components
#   - CSS custom properties for theming (dark/light, translucency)
#   - No inline styles except dynamic values (gauge angles, colors)
# - State management:
#   - Svelte 5 runes for local state
#   - Shared state via context or a typed store module
#   - No global mutable variables
# - Tauri bridge:
#   - All invoke() calls wrapped in typed async functions in src/lib/api/
#   - Return types match Rust command signatures exactly
#   - Handle errors at the call site — show user-friendly messages
# - Accessibility:
#   - ARIA labels on all interactive elements
#   - Keyboard navigation for the HUD overlay
#   - Sufficient contrast ratios even on translucent backgrounds

# ============================================================================
# ARCHITECTURE — CLEAN SEPARATION
# ============================================================================
# src-tauri/src/
#   providers/         — one module per AI tool (trait implementations)
#   polling/           — the engine that periodically fetches from providers
#   storage/           — SQLite repository (behind trait)
#   keychain/          — OS keychain access (behind trait)
#   commands/          — Tauri commands (thin layer, delegates to services)
#   models/            — shared types (Usage, Rate, Balance, Transaction)
#   errors/            — typed error hierarchy
#
# src/
#   lib/
#     api/             — typed Tauri invoke wrappers
#     components/
#       tray/          — menubar display components
#       hover/         — hover popover breakdown
#       hud/           — full translucent overlay
#       gauges/        — tachometer dials, sparklines, charts
#       shared/        — buttons, badges, icons
#     stores/          — reactive state (Svelte 5 runes)
#     types/           — TypeScript interfaces matching Rust models
#   app.html
#   routes/
#
# tests/
#   e2e/               — end-to-end tests (Playwright or WebdriverIO)
#   fixtures/          — mock API responses per provider

# ============================================================================
# CODE QUALITY GATES
# ============================================================================
# Before ANY code is considered complete:
#   [ ] Tests written FIRST (TDD red phase)
#   [ ] Tests pass (TDD green phase)
#   [ ] Code refactored with tests still green (TDD refactor phase)
#   [ ] No compiler warnings (Rust: deny(warnings), TS: strict)
#   [ ] No clippy warnings: cargo clippy -- -D warnings
#   [ ] Formatted: cargo fmt && npx prettier --write .
#   [ ] No secrets in code, logs, or comments
#   [ ] Error states handled — not ignored, not panicked
#   [ ] New public APIs have doc comments
#   [ ] Interface segregation verified — no bloated traits or prop types

# ============================================================================
# WHAT NOT TO DO
# ============================================================================
# - Do NOT write implementation before tests
# - Do NOT use any/unknown types in TypeScript
# - Do NOT store API keys anywhere except OS keychain
# - Do NOT log sensitive data (keys, tokens, auth headers)
# - Do NOT make synchronous HTTP calls
# - Do NOT create god-objects or god-traits
# - Do NOT pass entire data models when a subset will do
# - Do NOT ignore errors with _ = or .ok()
# - Do NOT use string-typed errors
# - Do NOT add dependencies without justification
# - Do NOT write tests that depend on execution order
# - Do NOT mock what you can test directly
# - Do NOT commit without running the full test suite
